// LEDGER item 3, 10th investigation: SOURCE CHANGE REVERTED after new
// evidence -- read tools/loop/LEDGER.md item 3 in full before attempting
// an 11th. Still NOT FIXED. Do not re-attempt the naive one-line
// consume_call_args change without ALSO closing every gap below; each
// looked like the last one until the next repro was built.
//
// Passing a struct that has HEAP FIELDS to any function -- a free function
// or a method, it makes no difference -- leaks that struct's heap body.
// Everything else in this file is FLAT.
//
// Measured peak RSS (Windows, --release), 250k vs 1M iterations, HEAD:
//
//   bare_array           4.4 MB -> 3.9 MB    flat   (no struct at all)
//   scalar_method        4.0 MB -> 3.9 MB    flat   (struct, but no heap field)
//   heap_field_direct    4.5 MB -> 4.5 MB    flat   (heap fields, NO call --
//                                              re-measured 2026-08-28: ALSO
//                                              leaks, 91.2 MB/1M, under a
//                                              separate, independent bug
//                                              this session found but did
//                                              NOT ship a fix for either --
//                                              see LEDGER item 3)
//   heap_field_method   25.7 MB -> 95.5 MB   LEAKS
//   free_fn_scalar_ret  11.8 MB -> 84.8 MB   LEAKS  (a free fn leaks the same)
//   method_chain        72.7 MB -> 279.5 MB  LEAKS  (each link leaks)
//
// WHY IT IS STILL OPEN, as of the 10th investigation (2026-08-28). The
// naive fix (add MirType::Struct to consume_call_args's Str/Array/Map
// borrow allowlist) makes the caller's struct local get a real scope-end
// Drop again -- correct in isolation -- but every backend's struct-drop
// path assumes a struct value has exactly ONE owner, and there turn out to
// be at least FOUR distinct, independent MIR/codegen shapes where a struct
// PICKS UP A SECOND ALIAS with no retain to match, each needing its own
// fix, each found only by testing the shape before it:
//
//   1. A method returns `self` bare (`SpinLock.lock() -> SpinLock { ...
//      return self }`). Cranelift boxes every struct uniformly, so the
//      destination local aliases the caller's identical box. FIXABLE (a
//      Cranelift-only retain at Terminator::Return + owner-count-checked
//      release, mirroring the existing boxed-array-element drop path) --
//      implemented and proven both ways this session
//      (tests/conformance/conf_spinlock_seq.kry pins the repro).
//   2. The SAME shape, but the struct ALSO has heap (str/array) fields:
//      LLVM's byval/sret struct copy is only shallow, so even though LLVM
//      never boxes the top-level struct, the caller's and destination's
//      copies still alias the SAME leaf str/array pointers. Needs a
//      SEPARATE, LLVM-only field-content retain at the same return site
//      (Cranelift's box-level retain doesn't help LLVM at all). Also
//      implemented and proven this session
//      (tests/mem/struct_selfreturn_leak.kry, `single_selfreturn` mode).
//   3. A struct PARAMETER is plainly REBOUND to a new named local before
//      being threaded onward (`let mut np = p; np.field = ..; return np`
//      -- the idiom the self-host parser is written in throughout:
//      `advance`/`expect`/`alloc_node`-style helpers). This is a DIFFERENT
//      MIR shape from #1 (a `let`, not a `return`) and needs its OWN
//      Cranelift-only retain at that binding site. Also implemented this
//      session.
//   4. Reading a STRUCT element out of an array into a NAMED local
//      (`let t = p.toks[p.pos]`) is -- BY EXISTING, DELIBERATE DESIGN,
//      predating this session -- an ALIAS, not an owned copy (Cranelift
//      codegen.rs's own RValue::Index comment: "nothing in MIR's
//      drop-tracking expects an Index-read temp to own fresh heap
//      memory"). That assumption is true for UNNAMED temps (never
//      dropped) but FALSE for a NAMED local -- `emit_named_scope_drops`
//      drops every named local unconditionally regardless of type. Before
//      this session, structs were never actually freed at all (this
//      item's own leak), so this pre-existing bug never surfaced; fixing
//      #1-#3 above made struct drops real again, which is what finally
//      exposed it as a live double-free
//      (compiler/self-host/regression_lexer_reentrant_tokenize.kry,
//      `Token` elements read out of a `[Token]` array field via `p.toks
//      [p.pos]` inside a `cur_text`-style helper).
//
// #4 was found DURING verification of #1-#3, not before -- i.e. every
// single one of these was invisible until the fix for the previous one
// made it real. NOT fixed this session: found with the self-host bootstrap
// still running, no time left to design, implement, and independently
// verify a correct treatment (retain-on-index-into-a-named-local, or
// excluding such a local from the unconditional named-scope drop) without
// risking a FIFTH undiscovered shape. This confirms, with hard evidence
// this time instead of a discovered-mid-attempt correction, what the 8th
// investigation's design note already concluded in the abstract: "closing
// this needs the drop helpers, container reads, spawn capture and
// reassignment converted together... not an incremental patch." All
// source changes for #1-#4 were reverted; `git diff` on lower.rs and both
// codegen.rs files is empty at HEAD. See tools/loop/LEDGER.md item 3 for
// the full account -- including a separate, independent, previously-
// undocumented leak mechanism found (but NOT fixed/shipped, same reason
// as above: no time left to re-verify it alone after the crash-fix path
// it was found alongside had to be reverted) and a precise, ready-to-
// implement fix for it -- and the three regression files
// (conf_spinlock_seq.kry, struct_selfreturn_leak.kry,
// regression_lexer_reentrant_tokenize.kry) now pinned for whoever
// attempts an 11th investigation, each proven to reproduce ITS OWN
// shape under the naive+partial fixes before being reverted.
//
// Run:  LEAK_MODE=<mode> LEAK_ITERS=<n> ./struct_arg_leak
// Modes: bare_array scalar_method heap_field_direct heap_field_method
//        free_fn_scalar_ret method_chain

struct Bag { items: [str], name: str }
impl Bag {
    fn size(self: Bag) -> i64 { return len(self.items) }
    fn add(self: Bag, s: str) -> Bag {
        return Bag { items: push(self.items, s), name: self.name }
    }
}

struct Plain { a: i64, b: i64 }
impl Plain {
    fn sum(self: Plain) -> i64 { return self.a + self.b }
}

fn size_fn(b: Bag) -> i64 { return len(b.items) }

@capabilities(process)
fn main() {
    let mode = env_get("LEAK_MODE")
    let raw = env_get("LEAK_ITERS")
    let iters = if len(raw) == 0 { 250000 } else { parse_int(raw) }

    let mut acc = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while i < iters {
        if mode == "bare_array" {
            let arr: [str] = ["p", "q"]
            acc = acc + len(arr)
        } elif mode == "scalar_method" {
            let p = Plain { a: 1, b: 2 }
            acc = acc + p.sum()
        } elif mode == "heap_field_direct" {
            let b = Bag { items: ["p", "q"], name: "n" }
            acc = acc + len(b.items)
        } elif mode == "heap_field_method" {
            let b = Bag { items: ["p", "q"], name: "n" }
            acc = acc + b.size()
        } elif mode == "free_fn_scalar_ret" {
            let b = Bag { items: ["p", "q"], name: "n" }
            acc = acc + size_fn(b)
        } elif mode == "method_chain" {
            let b = Bag { items: [], name: "n" }
            acc = acc + b.add("x").add("y").size()
        } else {
            println("unknown LEAK_MODE: " + mode)
            exit(2)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    println(mode + " iters=" + to_string(iters) + " acc=" + to_string(acc))
}
